<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Thaton Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">850 CE – 1057 CE (approx.) · capital Thaton</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Mon kingdom of Lower Myanmar, remembered as a centre of Theravada Buddhism whose conquest by Pagan in 1057 brought Mon learning to the Burmans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Thaton, near the Gulf of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Martaban</a>, appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> and Burmese tradition as the capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suphannaphum">Suvarnabhumi</a>, the 'golden land' of Buddhist legend. Inscriptions and archaeology suggest a Mon Buddhist town of the later first millennium, though the scale of its kingdom and its early history are uncertain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Thaton was a port and religious centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a>-speaking region of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Ramannadesa</a>. It was famed for its monks and Pali scriptures, and Burmese chronicles credit the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shin-arahan">Shin Arahan</a>, said to have come from Thaton, with converting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> of Pagan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>. Its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuha">Manuha</a>, is the best known ruler.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to the chronicles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom</a> attacked Thaton in 1057 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuha">Manuha</a> refused to give up Buddhist scriptures, and carried off the king, monks and craftsmen. Manuha's captivity is recalled by the Manuha temple at Bagan. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> culture revived later under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 850 CE – 1057 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:76.62%;width:4.12%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>850 CE – 1057 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thaton</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Myanmar</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Thaton, Suvarnabhumi (Mon), Ramannadesa (early)</dd></dl></aside></div>

